WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.240
You talk about teaching your kids to

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:07.520
struggle with grace and how you can

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.920
begin to inculcate the entrepreneur and

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.000
the embrace the struggle mindset really

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.240
early. Uh I've seen some examples you

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.320
posted on X about things you've done

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.000
with your kids and man people get mad

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.960
when you post any of the things where

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.800
you let a kid struggle. talk to me a bit

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.320
about like give me a story about whe

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.359
when you let a child struggle with

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.760
grace, what you hope they learned from

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.640
it and why you think more parents should

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.320
think about this.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.840
>> So when I when I was running Storage

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.600
Squad, our client was not the student.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.840
Even though we were picking up students

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.079
stuff, 80% of the people that we dealt

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.079
with were the parents.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:45.680
>> Meaning we did student storage. We we

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:46.879
were in these kids' dorms picking up

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.079
their stuff, storing it for the summer,

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:50.960
and taking it back to them wherever they

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:54.160
moved. You would think a college kid

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.760
aged 17 to 23, you would think that they

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.160
would be mature enough to get on their

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.920
computer, set up their appointment,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.120
answer the phone, call, you know, bring

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.039
their stuff downstairs, whatever it

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.439
might be. 80% of the people we dealt

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:08.560
with were parents. Their kids were in

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:10.000
their rooms playing computer games or

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.200
studying or sitting on the couch

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.880
watching TV while their mother was on

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.920
the phone with us scheduling their

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.560
appointments. Okay, that's just a fact.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.320
Across 7,000 customers, 80% of the

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.640
people we dealt with were their parents.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.560
That is a sad reality in so many

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.880
different areas of life that these kids,

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.960
they never build the muscle of getting

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.799
uncomfortable because their parents put

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.400
them in a bubble and take care of

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:39.520
everything for them. Okay? Like, I sent

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.759
my kid into Chick-fil-A to order for my

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.600
seven-year-old to order for my family. I

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:44.560
I my wife walked in first, told him it

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.159
was going to happen just so that they

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.079
didn't get a little freaked out. But he

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.360
walked in, he ordered, he paid, and he

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:51.119
brought the takeout out to the car, and

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.560
he was really, really proud of himself.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:54.240
When we're at the golf course, we're at

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.600
the halfway house, he wants some candy.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.840
He's a little bit shy. He doesn't want

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.600
to talk to the lady. He doesn't get the

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.320
candy, so he's got to talk to the lady,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.079
right? Um, even in Atlanta airport, we

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.200
were we showed up super early. We were

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:06.719
just having some fun. We were rolling

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.520
our suitcases. He had a ball with it,

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:11.599
but my he was five at the time. I had

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.120
him walk up to people in the TSA outfits

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:14.319
and ask them for directions on how to

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.800
get to our gate and he had his little

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:19.120
printed out ticket and we had a ball and

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.480
he got on the train himself. He went all

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.480
the way to the gate all by himself. I

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.879
didn't help him one time. I posted that

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.000
on X and uh

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:26.400
>> people are like, "You're a terrible

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.080
dad."

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.120
>> Yeah. They they they said I was abusive

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.800
and it was it was crazy. So I I think if

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.440
your kids don't get practice making

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.760
decisions and frankly doing a little bit

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.800
of uncomfortable things, they're not

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.319
there's no way that they can be

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.840
competent adults. But now my

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:45.040
seven-year-old can look an adult in the

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.879
eye. He can have a conversation. He can,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.400
you know, obviously he's not capable of

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.760
making many decisions, but he can do

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.680
some things. [laughter]

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.760
He does he I I'm not going to brush his

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.920
teeth or bathe him in the bath at 7

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.800
years old like like a surprising amount

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.440
of parents actually do. I I I had no

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.200
idea. So I do like my my daughters,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.200
they're twins, they're five right now.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.720
Um they they love dogs they see on the

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.640
street because of course you do. And

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.120
I've drilled into them. You always must

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.159
ask. You must ask the dog. May I pet the

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.080
dog? You know

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:17.760
what French? Okay, fine. Let's go. Yes.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.360
And sometimes the answer is no.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.120
>> And then you must respect it because

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.040
some dogs will bite

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.120
>> and like that that gives them the same

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.800
idea, right? Talk to an adult, have it

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.480
engage. And of course, adults are

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.840
charmed when two little 5-year-old girls

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.599
come up and ask you, "Do you pet your

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.440
dog?" And if the dog is a good dog, they

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:35.680
will always say yes, because of course

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.959
you will. But that's the same idea. You

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.760
teach the child. Or last week, I gave

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:43.280
one of my twin daughters uh $5 and said,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.480
"Go to the corner store because here in

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.680
Montreal, like almost every corner

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.200
there's a store. You don't have to cross

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.200
the street. I don't want a 5-year-old

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.560
crossing the street yet." Um and just

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.519
like, "We need tortilla chips for

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.959
dinner. Come back with tortilla chips.

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:56.879
came back with potato chip which is not

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:58.560
quite the right thing like she can't

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:00.080
lead yet but she's like I know what

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.319
tortilla chips are I will get the right

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.159
ones out of you and I went I believe you

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:05.599
and I'm like well guys are not getting

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:07.040
tortilla chip with dinner tonight we'll

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.040
just have tacos instead

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.280
>> that's amazing that it and there's so

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.560
many I I love parenting I'm super

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.920
passionate about this and I hope you ask

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.160
more questions because I can talk all

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.880
day about it but but there are so many

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.320
similarities to entrepreneurship

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.759
>> if your employees come to you with a

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.759
problem and you solve it for them every

00:04:21.759 --> 00:04:25.680
single time. What do you think they're

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:28.000
going to do when a problem comes?

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.520
They're going to keep coming to you and

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.240
they are never going to build that

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.240
muscle of making decisions. So

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.160
>> a common like it's a very common theme

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.000
of like enable your kids to make some

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.680
decisions and learn to do some things

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.040
and so many people also put their

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.400
employees in bubbles and solve every

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.759
problem for them and they become the

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.360
bottleneck of their business so that

